==============================================================================
[E] D2 vs D3: the split is nu-AVERAGED, so D3 suffices for the assembly
==============================================================================
  toy: g(U') = exp(3 tanh U'),  nu = N(0,1)
    sup_U' g(U')   =   20.07165     <- D2 must bound THIS (pointwise)
    E_nu[g]        =    3.82120     <- D3 must bound only THIS (averaged)
    ratio E/sup    =    0.19038     (strict, and can be made 0)
  two-scale law of total variance:
    Var_mu(F) = E_nu[ Var_{mu(.|U')}(F) ]  +  Var_nu( E_mu[F|U'] )
    term1 is E_nu[.]  =>  a nu-averaged fiber LSI (D3) bounds term1 EXACTLY.
    term2 is coarse  =>  H1 bounds it.
  => D3 (nu-averaged) is SUFFICIENT for  H1 & D3 => Var_mu(F) <= max(1/lam,1/lam_f) E|grad F|^2
  => D2 (pointwise) is STRICTLY stronger than D3, and buys ONLY the pointwise-in-U' form
     that (star dec)/L3-band-decay needs (S4 Sec.3.5), NOT the UC-COMPLETE three candidates.

